# freshness-tie.nix — ONE shape for "this generated artifact still describes
# the source it was generated from".
#
# ── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A generated artifact that outlives its source fix, with nothing tying the
# two, is a recurring defect class rather than an incident. Four independent
# instances inside one night (2026-08-01):
#
#   1. `aresta` — Cargo.toml fixed 93f8fb8 (2026-07-31); the committed
#      crate2nix `Cargo.nix` last regenerated 6eb086b (2026-07-18). Its
#      `flake.nix` imports that file to build the SHIPPED FIPS sidecar
#      image, so the image linked `aws-lc-fips-sys 0.13.14` while
#      `Cargo.lock` resolved `0.13.16` — a validated algorithm against a
#      different module than the manifest names.
#   2. `pangea-operator` — `Cargo.toml` edited, then `gen build` regenerated
#      `Cargo.gen.lock` against the PRE-refresh `Cargo.lock` and reported
#      its own tie OK. Both sides agreed on the wrong thing.
#   3. `pangea-operator` CRDs — new status fields would have been dropped by
#      the API server had `deploy/crds/*.yaml` not been regenerated by hand.
#   4. `blue` — `checks.gen-confirm` green having verified nothing, because
#      `--if-present` tolerates a repo with no committed delta.
#
# `Cargo.gen.lock` was the one artifact of the four that HAD a tie — the D2
# gate comparing a recorded `cargo_lock_sha256` against
# `hashFile "sha256" Cargo.lock` — and it worked: it is what caught (2).
# That gate existed TWICE, inline, in two files that had already drifted
# apart in wording. This file is the third use, so it is the extraction:
# `build/rust/lockfile-delta.nix` (hash tie), `build/go/lockfile-delta.nix`
# (hash tie) and `build/rust/cargo-nix-tie.nix` (derived-set tie) now share
# one message and one throw, and cannot disagree about what "stale" means.
#
# ── THE CONTRACT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `held` NEVER RETURNS FALSE. It returns `true` or throws. A tie that
# reports a boolean is a verdict the caller is free to drop on the floor,
# which is the same class of defect as the `--if-present` gate in (4) — see
# the ★★ `runTests` REPORTS section of CLAUDE.md. Callers force it with
# `builtins.seq`, so the throw lands before the artifact is used, not after.
#
# TIER: eval-rejected (a Nix `throw`). Stronger than a CI gate — it does not
# depend on Actions being enabled, which 554 of 716 workflow-bearing pleme-io
# repos are not — and weaker than truly-unrepresentable, because a `throw` is
# not a compile error. Do not round it up.
{ }:

{
  # `src` is a path; interpolating it identifies the repo (a
  # /nix/store/…-source for a flake input, a real path for a local
  # checkout). Guarded with tryEval so a pathological src degrades the
  # message rather than replacing the real error with a confusing
  # secondary one.
  hint =
    src:
    let
      r = builtins.tryEval (toString src);
    in
    if r.success then r.value else "<unprintable src>";

  # held :: { … } -> true | throw
  #
  #   subject  — the reporting module, e.g. "lockfile-delta(go)"
  #   artifact — the generated file that may be stale, e.g. "Cargo.nix"
  #   where    — already-stringified location of the workspace (`hint src`)
  #   fresh    — the tie itself, computed by the caller
  #   sides    — the two projections being compared, one per line, aligned
  #              by the caller (the reader must be able to see WHICH side
  #              moved without re-deriving anything)
  #   cause    — what moved, and why shipping it anyway is a defect
  #   fix      — the command, run in the workspace named above
  #   fleetFix — optional: how to find every drifted workspace at once,
  #              rather than one eval failure at a time
  #
  # NAME THE WORKSPACE. The predecessor of this message carried two hashes
  # and nothing else, and surfaced deep inside an unrelated consumer's
  # module trace (a stale `mado` took out `nix run .#rebuild` on cid via
  # nix-darwin -> assertions -> home-manager.sharedModules). Identifying the
  # culprit then meant hashing every Cargo.lock in the org — a five-minute
  # scan to recover information the throw site already held in `src`.
  held =
    {
      subject,
      artifact,
      where,
      fresh,
      sides,
      cause,
      fix,
      fleetFix ? null,
    }:
    if fresh then
      true
    else
      throw ''
        ${subject}: ${artifact} is STALE (freshness tie failed).
          workspace = ${where}
        ${builtins.concatStringsSep "\n" (map (s: "  ${s}") sides)}

        ${cause}

        Fix at the source, in the workspace named above:
          ${fix}

        Do NOT fix it by pinning the consumer back to an older revision —
        that hides a real break behind a stale pin.${
          if fleetFix == null then
            ""
          else
            ''

              To find every drifted workspace at once rather than one failure at a
              time:
                ${fleetFix}''
        }
      '';
}
